   383	        raise SystemExit(f"usage: python {sys.argv[0]} status <state_dir>")
   384	    print(describe_recovery(sys.argv[2]))
   385	    sys.exit(1 if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(os.path.realpath(sys.argv[2]), MANIFEST)) else 0)

README
     1	# records_txn — fail-closed record updates for Planner records scripts
     2	
     3	`records_txn.py` (standard library only, Python ≥ 3.9) is the shared helper that Codex review *Planner records
     4	scripts P1* asked for (BLOCKING 1–2, HIGH 1–2, NIT 2, pattern P5). A records script **declares** everything first,
     5	then calls `commit()` once. Any exception before `commit()` means nothing was written. Every refusal is an explicit
     6	`Refused` (a `SystemExit` subclass: exit 1, clean message, not swallowed by `except Exception`); there is no `assert`,
     7	so `python -O` changes nothing.
     8	
     9	## API
    10	
    11	```python
    12	import sys; sys.path.insert(0, "<dir of records_txn.py>")
    13	import records_txn as rt
    14	
    15	txn = rt.Transaction(state_dir=STATE)        # refuses if an unfinished transaction's recovery manifest exists
    16	txn.expect_date("2026-11-05")                # refuses unless the clock's America/New_York date is that day
    17	txn.iso, txn.hm                              # one clock read: '2026-11-05T18:30:00-05:00', '18:30 EST' (zone derived)
    18	rev = txn.require_text(REV, "<full sha256>", must_start="**Reviewer:** OpenAI Codex",
    19	                       must_contain=["**Round:** C3c", "**Verdict:** REQUEST-CHANGES"])   # verified str
    20	raw = txn.require_source(LOG, "<full sha256>")             # verified bytes; derive every recorded fact from these
    21	hand = txn.text(HAND)                        # destination text, read ONCE; optional 2nd arg pins its sha256
    22	txn.replace_once(HAND, old, new)             # refuses unless `old` occurs exactly once (overlaps counted)
    23	txn.append(NOTE, "\n## ...\n")               # refuses unless the file and the text both end with "\n"
    24	txn.create(NEW, data, parents=False)         # bytes or str; refuses if NEW exists (even as a dangling symlink)
    25	txn.copy_verified(SRC, DST, "<full sha256>", parents=True) # DST gets exactly the verified bytes
    26	done = txn.commit(verify=lambda: head_is_expected())       # returns the archived manifest (the audit record)
    27	```
    28	
    29	Several edits to one file chain in memory (each sees the previous one). Two spellings of one path are one
    30	destination. A file read with `text()` and left unchanged is still compare-and-swap checked, but never rewritten.
    31	A transaction is single-use: after `commit()` or any refusal, every further call refuses.
    32	
    33	## What `commit()` does (all checks before the first write)
    34	
    35	1. `flock(LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)` on `<state_dir>/records_txn.lock`; a second writer is refused, not queued.
    36	2. Refuse if a recovery manifest exists; re-read every destination: same sha256, still one hard link, still not a
    37	   symlink; every new file still absent. Then `verify()` runs under the lock and must return exactly `True`. Use it
    38	   to re-check branch / HEAD / origin, e.g. `git rev-parse HEAD` against a pinned SHA.
    39	3. Stage each output to `.<name>.<txid>.records_txn.tmp` in the destination's own directory (`O_CREAT|O_EXCL`),
    40	   with the old file's mode for replacements, fsync it and read it back. Then fsync the directories.
    41	4. Write `<state_dir>/records_txn.recovery.json` (`O_EXCL`; every planned install with old / new sha256, the
    42	   verified sources, created directories, argv, host, pid) and fsync it and the state directory.
    43	5. Take a last look (every destination unchanged), then install, new files first: `link(2)` for new files (it can
    44	   never overwrite), and a per-file re-check plus `rename(2)` for edits. Fsync the directories.
    45	6. Archive the manifest to `<state_dir>/done/<txid>.json` and clear the recovery mark.
    46	
    47	Failure before the first install removes every temp, the manifest and every created directory: each destination is
    48	byte-identical. After that point the helper raises `RecoveryNeeded` and keeps the manifest and the remaining temps.
    49	
    50	## Recovery (never automatic)
    51	
    52	`python records_txn.py status <state_dir>` (exit 1 while unfinished) lists each planned install as
    53	`[installed]`, `[pending]` (destination still old, staged temp complete) or `[CONFLICT]`. Finish a pending
    54	replacement with `mv TEMP DST`, a pending new file with `ln TEMP DST && rm TEMP`, or discard it with `rm TEMP`.
    55	Restore installed entries from git if the whole set must go. Then move the manifest to `<state_dir>/done/`. Re-run
    56	the script: its anchors re-validate against whatever is now on disk.
    57	
    58	## Conventions for records scripts
    59	
    60	- Pin every source by its **full** sha256 (`sha256sum`; prefixes and `…` are refused). Historic event times and
    61	  verdicts come from those sources, not from typed literals. Never append "EST" yourself: `txn.hm` carries the zone.
    62	- Use **one** `state_dir` for every script that touches the same records, outside any git repo, on a local
    63	  filesystem with hard links (create it once with `mkdir -p`). A different `state_dir` means a different lock.
    64	- Run git (`pull --rebase`, `commit`, `push`) only before the script starts or after it finishes, and tee those
    65	  commands to a log. The helper does not run git.
    66	
    67	## Limits
    68	
    69	- The lock binds only `records_txn` users of the same `state_dir`. An editor or git writing a destination is
    70	  caught by the compare-and-swap checks up to the final window between a file's last check and its `rename(2)`.
    71	  Linux has no compare-and-swap rename.
    72	- The install is not atomic across files. A crash in step 5 leaves a recovery-marked, refused state, never a silent
    73	  partial one. Destinations must be UTF-8 regular files; sources and copies are read whole into memory. Mode is
    74	  kept; owner, ACLs and xattrs are not.
    75	
    76	Tests: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES= <flac python> -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider <this dir>` (tmp_path only).

